\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}(1-({2}^{5})^{1/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}(1-4\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}(4\\sqrt{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538226 17592186576411 false] [17592186284676 1074 "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =2-{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =-3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =-3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u & ={x}^{2}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2-{x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{(2-{x}^{3})}^{3}}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{2}^{1}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\text{d}u & =-\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{1}{u}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}|}_{2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}({1}^{5/2}-{2}^{5/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}(1-({2}^{5})^{1/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}(1-4\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}(4\\sqrt{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538226 17592186576411 true] [17592186284678 1074 "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorn