0}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-{105}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}-{150}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{150}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{6}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538226 17592186576411 true] [17592186284693 1071 "Simplify the expression ${[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})]}^{16}.$" 13194139538226 17592186576411 false] [17592186284693 1071 "Simplify the expression ${[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)]}^{16}.$" 13194139538226 17592186576411 true] [17592186284693 1074 "For expressions involving subtraction, De Moivre's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )]}^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=16$ and $\\phi =\\frac{\\theta }{2}.$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})]}^{16} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(16\\cdot \\frac{\\theta }{2})-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16\\cdot \\frac{\\theta }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538226 17592186576411 false] [17592186284693 1074 "For expressions involving subtraction, De Moivre's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )]}^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=16$ and $\\phi =\\frac{\\theta }{2}.$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)]}^{16} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(16\\cdot \\frac{\\theta }{2})-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16\\cdot \\frac{\\theta }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538226 17592186576411 true] [17592186284694 1074 "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})={30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/8c/8c6e3ae6bdbfb1c66aee719f77225453992fffe6f3c6e3dfdc3f1ff92c59863a.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={330}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={330}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139538226 17592186576411 false] [17592186284694 1074 "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)={30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/8c/8c