ur integral in terms of $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{(3-{x}^{2})}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {(3-{x}^{2})}^{5}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{5}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{6}}{6}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}{u}^{6}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}{(3-{x}^{2})}^{6}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{{(3-{x}^{2})}^{6}}{12}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538226 17592186576411 false] [17592186290504 1074 "In this case, the $x$ that's outside the parentheses isn't quite the derivative of $(3-{x}^{2}).$ But the derivative is $-2x,$ so we're only out by a constant factor. \n\nSo we make the substitution\n\n$$\nu=3-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x\\,⟹\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can now write our integral in terms of $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{(3-{x}^{2})}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {(3-{x}^{2})}^{5}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{5}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{6}}{6}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}{u}^{6}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}{(3-{x}^{2})}^{6}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{{(3-{x}^{2})}^{6}}{12}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538226 17592186576411 true] [17592186290518 1074 "We note that $10x$ is the derivative of the quantity in the square root. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu=5{x}^{2}-4.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\