ts associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4)+5\\cdot f(5) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{3}{10})+1(\\frac{9}{50})+2(\\frac{4}{25})+3(\\frac{7}{50})+4(\\frac{3}{25})+5(\\frac{1}{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{50}+\\frac{8}{25}+\\frac{21}{50}+\\frac{12}{25}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{50}+\\frac{16}{50}+\\frac{21}{50}+\\frac{24}{50}+\\frac{25}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+16+21+24+25}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{95}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538226 17592186576411 false] [17592186292920 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4)+5\\cdot f(5) \\\\  & =0\\left(\\frac{3}{10}\\right)+1\\left(\\frac{9}{50}\\right)+2\\left(\\frac{4}{25}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{7}{50}\\right)+4\\left(\\frac{3}{25}\\right)+5\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{50}+\\frac{8}{25}+\\frac{21}{50}+\\frac{12}{25}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{50}+\\frac{16}{50}+\\frac{21}{50}+\\frac{24}{50}+\\frac{25}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+16+21+24+25}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{95}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538226 17592186576411 true] [17592186292921 1074 "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single roll of the game. On each roll in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If rolling gives an odd number ($1$, $3,$ or $5$), the player loses $$2,$ so we have $p=-2.$\n\n- If rolling gives an even number ($2$ or $4$), the player wins $$10,$ so we have $p=10.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\nP(-2)=\\frac{3}{5},\\,P(10)=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $-2$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[p] & =(-2)\\cdot f(-2)+10\\cdot f(10) \\\\  & =(-2)(\\frac{3}{5})+10(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{20}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{5} \\\\  & =2.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $$2.80.$" 13194139538226 17592186576411 false] [17592186292921 1074 "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single roll of the game. On each roll in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If rolling gives an odd number ($1$, $3,$ or $5$), the player loses $$2,$ so we have $p=-2.$\n\n- If rolling gives an even number ($2$ or $4$), the player wins $$10,$ so we have $p=10.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\nP(-2)=\\frac{3}{5},\\,P(10)=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $-2$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[p] & =(-2)\\cdot f(-2)+10\\cdot f(10) \\\\  & =(-2)\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)+10\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{20}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{5} \\\\  & =2.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $$2.80.$" 13194139538226 17592186576411 true] [17592186292922 1074 "First of all, profit is revenue minus cost, or in our case, winnings minus cost. So, once we have found the expected winnings after twenty games, we need to subtract $10\\cdot 20=200$ cents to account for the price of playing.\n\nNow, let's find the expected winnings. For each play, the five coins have an equal chance of being dispensed, so each one has a probability of $\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table. If $W$ is the amount of winnings (in cents) on a single game, and $f(w)$ is the probability mass function for $W,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(w)$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $w$ | $1$ | $5$ | $10$ | $25$ | $50$ |\n\nThe expected value of $W$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =1\\cdot f(1)+5\\cdot f(5)+10\\cdot f(10)+25\\cdot f(25)+50\\cdot f(50) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{5})+5(\\frac{1}{5})+10(\\frac{1}{5})+25(\\frac{1}{5})+50(\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{5}{5}+\\frac{10}{5}+\\frac{25}{5}+\\frac{50}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+5+10+25+50}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{91}{5} \\\\  & =18.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the expected winnings for a single game. So, the expected winnings for $20$ games is\n\n$$\n20\\cdot \\text{E}[W]=20\\cdot 18.2=364.\n$$\n\nNow, subtracting the $200$ cents paid to play, we get that the expected profit (in cents) is\n\n$$\nE[p]=364-200=164.\n$$" 13194139538226 17592186576411 false] [17592186292922 1074 "First of all, profit is revenue minus cost, or in our case, winnings minus cost. So, once we have found the expected winnings after twenty games, we need to subtract $10\\cdot 20=200$ cents to account for the price of playing.\n\nNow, let's find the expected winnings. For each play, the five coins have an equal chance of being dispensed, so each one has a probability of $\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table. If $W$ is the amount of winnings (in cents) on a single game, and $f(w)$ is the probability mass function for $W,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(w)$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $w$ | $1$ | $5$ | $10$ | $25$ | $50$ |\n\nThe expected value of $W$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =1\\cdot f(1)+5\\cdot f(5)+10\\cdot f(10)+25\\cdot f(25)+50\\cdot f(50) \\\\  & =1\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)+5\\lef