 the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>11)=0.20.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 11) & =1-P(X>11) \\\\  & =1-0.20 \\\\  & =0.80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 11) & =0.80 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{11-8}{\\sigma }) & =0.80 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{3}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.80 \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.80)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.80)=0.8416.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sigma } & =0.8416 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{3}{0.8416} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈3.5646.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X\\le 6)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{6-8}{3.5646}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -0.56) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.56) \\\\  & =0.2877 \\\\  & ≈28.8%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $28.8%$ of laptops will last less than $6$ hours on a single charge." 13194139538226 17592186576411 true] [17592186292930 1074 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=8x-1 \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2x}-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-1 & =\\frac{1}{2x}-1 \\\\ 8x & =\\frac{1}{2x} \\\\ 16{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8x-1 \\\\  & =8(-\\frac{1}{4})-1 \\\\  & =-2-1 \\\\  & =-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-\\frac{1}{4},-3).$\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8x-1 \\\\  & =8(\\frac{1}{4})-1 \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(\\frac{1}{4},1).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $A(-\\frac{1}{4},-3)$ and $B(\\frac{1}{4},1).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/bb/bb69eb0150879f215fe453e596ae18273ec149f1d1486c8d7dfe7b2c1e3e5e58.png)" 13194139538226 17592186576411 false] [17592186292930 1074 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=8x-1 \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2x}-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-1 & =\\frac{1}{2x}-1 \\\\ 8x & =\\frac{1}{2x} \\\\ 16{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8x-1 \\\\  & =8\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-1 \\\\  & =-2-1 \\\\  & =-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-\\frac{1}{4},-3).$\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8x-1 \\\\  & =8\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-1 \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(\\frac{1}{4},1).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $A(-\\frac{1}{4},-3)$ and $B(\\frac{1}{4},1).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/bb/bb69eb0150879f215fe453e596ae18273ec149f1d1486c8d7dfe7b2c1e3e5e58.png)" 13194139538226 17592186576411 true] [17592186292931 1074 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{2}{x}-5 \\\\ y &=8x+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-5 & =8x+1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & =8x+6 \\\\ 2 & =